<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<!--
  x86-32 Watcom compiler spec (watcall) — a BEYOND-GHIDRA extension. No Ghidra x86 processor
  ships a watcom cspec (verified: x86/data/languages has gcc/win/borland/delphi/golang/swift,
  none watcom). This models the default Watcom C/C++ 32-bit register calling convention
  (`__watcall`), grounded in Open Watcom source + docs (not memory):

    - Argument registers, in order: EAX, EDX, EBX, ECX, then the stack. Grounded in
      open-watcom-v2 `bld/watcom/h/owflat.h:519` (`__parm [__eax] [__edx] [__ebx] [__ecx]`)
      and `bld/wasm/c/asmins.c:935` (`{ "eax", "edx", "ebx", "ecx" }`).
    - Integer/pointer return in EAX (docs/doc/cg/cpwcc.gml: a struct return passes a pointer
      in EAX; a scalar returns in AX/EAX).
    - Caller-saved (killed by call): EAX ONLY.
    - Callee-saved (preserved / unaffected): ECX, EDX, EBX, ESI, EDI, EBP, ESP (and DF) — the
      argument registers included. A register carrying an argument is still restored by the
      callee under this convention.

      ⚠️ THIS LIST PREVIOUSLY READ "EAX, ECX, EDX", justified by a REAL quote from
      cpwcc.gml — "registers EAX, ECX and EDX are not saved and restored when a call is made".
      That quote is correct but describes the WRONG CONVENTION: it appears at cpwcc.gml:1785
      and :1813, inside the STACK-based convention notes ("Arguments are pushed on the stack
      from right to left … The called routine will remove the arguments"), i.e. __stdcall-like
      and __syscall; the occurrence at :1521 is in the __cdecl block. This spec models
      __watcall, the REGISTER convention. Do not re-derive the old list from that sentence.

      The register convention is grounded in the COMPILER'S OWN OUTPUT instead (self-compiled
      wcc386 -bt=linux -s -oc, objdump; see oracle/ground-truth/src/callclob.c and its gate):

        * a loop counter is kept LIVE ACROSS AN INDIRECT CALL, never spilled — impossible if
          the callee could destroy it. With 1/2/4 arguments the counter lands in EDX/ECX/ESI
          respectively: the argument count changes WHICH register is chosen, never the
          assumption that it survives.
        * the decisive evidence is on the CALLEE side — a function that uses EDX emits
          `push edx` in its prologue (`53 51 52 56 57 55` = ebx,ecx,edx,esi,edi,ebp). It
          preserves EDX for its caller, which is what callee-saved means. Every function
          observed also pushes ECX when it uses it.
        * only EAX is left unpreserved, and EAX is the return register.

      Corroborated independently by warcraft2-re/analysis/toolchain.md (~line 590): WAR2
      functions save ECX in their prologues project-wide, implying a
      `#pragma aux DEFAULT … modify exact [eax]` build — everything preserved except EAX.

      The symptom of the old list was WRONG CODE: a spurious killedbycall on the counter's
      register made Heritage::guardCalls insert an INDIRECT that took over the loop phi's tail
      input, so the update's only surviving consumer became a call argument and the loop
      emitted with no update at all — a loop that cannot terminate.

  Register-convention symbols carry a *trailing* underscore (`foo_`) vs the leading underscore
  of the stack `__cdecl` convention (cpwcc.gml) — a symbol-decoration detail, not part of the
  storage model below.

  Structure mirrors Ghidra's own register-convention cspec x86borland.cspec (__fastcall);
  the data organization is the shared i386 flat model.
-->
<compiler_spec>
  <data_organization>
     <absolute_max_alignment value="0" />
     <machine_alignment value="2" />
     <default_alignment value="1" />
     <default_pointer_alignment value="4" />
     <pointer_size value="4" />
     <wchar_size value="2" />
     <short_size value="2" />
     <integer_size value="4" />
     <long_size value="4" />
     <long_long_size value="8" />
     <float_size value="4" />
     <double_size value="8" />
     <long_double_size value="8" />
     <size_alignment_map>
          <entry size="1" alignment="1" />
          <entry size="2" alignment="2" />
          <entry size="4" alignment="4" />
          <entry size="8" alignment="4" />
     </size_alignment_map>
  </data_organization>
  <global>
    <range space="ram"/>
  </global>
  <stackpointer register="ESP" space="ram"/>
  <default_proto>
    <prototype name="__watcall" extrapop="unknown" stackshift="4">
      <input>
        <pentry minsize="1" maxsize="4">
          <register name="EAX"/>
        </pentry>
        <pentry minsize="1" maxsize="4">
          <register name="EDX"/>
        </pentry>
        <pentry minsize="1" maxsize="4">
          <register name="EBX"/>
        </pentry>
        <pentry minsize="1" maxsize="4">
          <register name="ECX"/>
        </pentry>
        <pentry minsize="1" maxsize="500" align="4">
          <addr offset="4" space="stack"/>
        </pentry>
      </input>
      <output>
        <pentry minsize="1" maxsize="4">
          <register name="EAX"/>
        </pentry>
      </output>
      <unaffected>
        <register name="ESP"/>
        <register name="EBP"/>
        <register name="ESI"/>
        <register name="EDI"/>
        <register name="DF"/>
            </unaffected>
      <killedbycall>
        <register name="EAX"/>
        <register name="EDX"/>
        <register name="EBX"/>
        <register name="ECX"/>
      </killedbycall>
    </prototype>
  </default_proto>
</compiler_spec>
